  <main>
    <article class="article">
      <h1 class="section-title">Faith Kipyegon: Consistency and the return to the top</h1>
      <p class="article-meta"><svg style="width:1em;height:1em;vertical-align:-0.15em;margin-right:0.3em" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M16 2v4M8 2v4M3 10h18"/></svg>7 July 2026 · <span class="accent"><svg style="width:1em;height:1em;vertical-align:-0.15em;margin-right:0.25em" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2l2.9 6.26 6.88.56-5.2 4.52 1.56 6.7L12 17.27 5.86 20.54l1.56-6.7-5.2-4.52 6.88-.56z"/></svg>Champions</span> · 3 min read</p>

      <picture>
        <source type="image/avif" srcset="images/cat-champions.avif?v=1">
        <source type="image/webp" srcset="images/cat-champions.webp?v=1">
        <img src="images/cat-champions.jpg?v=1" alt="Running shoe laces: a symbol of consistency in running" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="740" height="494">
      </picture>
      <p>
        <strong>Faith Kipyegon</strong> has been at the top of the 1500m for more than ten years.
        Lasting that long in such a fast event is rare. To understand how she did it, it's worth
        looking at her path in order.
      </p>

      <h3>The early years</h3>
      <ul class="career-timeline">
        <li><span class="year">1994</span>Born in Kenya. As a child she runs kilometres every day just to get to school.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2010</span>At 16 she becomes world junior cross-country champion.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2011</span>World youth champion in the 1500m.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The talent showed early. But plenty of kids win medals at 16 and then disappear. What came
        next, in her case, was slower.
      </p>

      <h3>The top</h3>
      <ul class="career-timeline">
        <li><span class="year">2015</span>Silver at the World Championships in Beijing, behind Genzebe Dibaba.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2016</span>First Olympic gold in the 1500m, in Rio.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2017</span>World champion in London.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The pause</h3>
      <ul class="career-timeline">
        <li><span class="year">2018</span>Her daughter Alyn is born. She misses the whole racing season.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2019</span>She comes back and takes silver at the World Championships in Doha.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is where many expected her career to start fading. She just needed a little time to find
        her rhythm again.
      </p>
      <!-- YOUR OWN LINE: If you've taken a break from running yourself (work, injury, family),
           what was coming back like? One or two sentences. -->

      <h3>Stronger than before</h3>
      <ul class="career-timeline">
        <li><span class="year">2021</span>Second Olympic gold in the 1500m, in Tokyo, in an Olympic record.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2022</span>World champion again, in Eugene.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2023</span>Within a few weeks she breaks three world records: 1500m, 5000m and the mile. At the World Championships in Budapest she wins gold at both distances.</li>
        <li><span class="year">2024</span>A third straight Olympic gold in the 1500m, in Paris. No one before her, man or woman, had done it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What I see in this list</h3>
      <p>
        Read it from top to bottom and it's hard to find a single year when everything changed. You
        see silvers before the golds, a year without racing and a lot of work in between. That's how
        our own progress comes too: one training cycle at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Try writing your own list, from the day you started running. Your first run without
        stopping, your first race, your first break. You'll see you've come further than you think.
      </p>

